urvives it all after all of that  he kept quiet for over 50 years and  finally wrote this memoir as a call to  arms to bring the japanese to account  for what they got up to during the war  it's  a difficult book to read at times but  really inspiring it's an amazing  reminder of just how bad life could  actually be and when i'm stuck in  traffic or annoyed that my internet's  running slow or pissed that the delivery  driver's gone next door it makes me  reset and actually focus on just  it does it matter think about some of  the challenges that people have actually  faced in life and um  yeah if i haven't got dysentery for  three and a half years solid  i reckon i'm doing all right by the way  if you want more suggestions i've just  completed a reading list of the 100 best  books that i've ever read so if you want  more awesome reads you can pick up a  copy for free link is on the screen  somewhere and in the description below  next up is lying by sam harris this is a  one hour read on why you should never  lie it's hugely transformational for me  over the last few years not because i  was lying all the time or something but  just that telling the truth is actually  quite difficult especially if you're not  working on it a lot of the time it's  easier to tell a lie than to tell the  truth and without a compelling reason  for why you shouldn't lie you just keep  on slipping down that so sam's book is a  really good insight into the nature of  lying why you do it and the negative  effects that it has on you  and others in my experience telling the  truth is like a superpower you never  have to remember what you said you never  have to be concerned about being found  out for something because what you did  always aligns with what you said you did  it really does alleviate a lot of  anxiety and it makes you a lot more  proud of the person that you are  especially if you've gone through your  20s perhaps doing some stuff that were  less than honorable at times there's no  reason not to pick this book up you can  read it in a single sitting  and it might just change your life next  up we've got the ape that understood the  universe by steve stewart williams as  far as i'm concerned evolutionary  psychology is the closest you're going  to get at peering under the hood of your  own behavior and discovering why you do  the things you do steve stuart williams  writes one of the easiest and most  accessible books on evolutionary  psychology that i've ever found and he  answers some unbelievable questions like  why are we attracted to certain traits  in the opposite sex or why is jealousy a  thing are we monogamous creatures why do  we help our family more than we help  strangers or why do we make alliances  with some people and enemies of others  as someone who doesn't like being at the  mercy of my programming becoming aware  of the evolutionary impulses that are  driving my behavior was super insightful  and very satisfying to me we can't  always transcend our nature but being  aware of it is an important first step  if you're interested in human nature and  why we are the way we are you'll be  fascinated by this like it's it's  basically just an endless barrage of  jaw-dropping discoveries and it's really  easy to read i found myself highlighting  pretty much  every other paragraph next up we've got  the precipice by toby odd which is  potentially the most important book on  this entire list it's the best insight  into existential risk that i've ever  found a complete breakdown of how  humanity could go extinct the relative  likelihoods of each risk occurring and  proposed solutions so from asteroids to  artificial intelligence bio weapons to  nuclear war this is a one-stop shop for  understanding the risks that our  civilization faces and what we can do  about it the precipice really did shake  my world view quite fundamentally and  reminded me that as a steward of the  globe right now i do have a duty to all  of the future generations that are going  to come to leave this a better world  than i found it it also gave me a sense  of awe and dread kind of like when you  look up at the night sky it makes you  feel insignificant in a beautiful way  and it stretches that out across an  entire book this  for some people that i've told to read  it has changed their life and it makes  me very happy that i genuinely think  those people are going to work very very  hard in a way that's going to make  everybody else's existence improved the  precipice is a very easy read it's super  accessible and it's even easier to  listen to actually because toby's got  this beautiful australian accent it  makes a nice change from reading  personal development all the time you  can improve the texture of your own  worldview without constantly looking for  a hack that directly relates to you this  is the biggest picture that you can get  and yet it's changed the way that i go  about my day pretty much all the time  next up we've got lost connections by  johan hari this is a fantastic dive into  depression's causes almost everyone is  going to suffer with some form of  depression during their adult lives so  educating yourself on it makes a lot of  sense johan has trolled the entire globe  speaking to some of the world's most  prominent experts and drawing on  examples from history anthropology  politics and a lot more to elaborate his  points for a long time depression was  judged as an imbalance of chemicals in  the brain and that the environmental  impact was actually very small johann  turns this on its head and reminds us  that unmet needs not unrequited  chemicals are what might be causing  depression it puts you back in control  and this is what i found very liberating  about it that it permitted me to do what  i always knew was right that i have  control over my mood on a day-to-day  basis and that there are certain  pathways that i need to make sure i'm  fulfilling before i can say i have a  problem with my brain it's like look are  you getting up at the right time each  day are you eating sleeping walking do  you have friends that care about you do  you have a job that you committed to do  you have a local community that you feel  a part of if you don't have any of these  things what are you doing talking about  the way that your brain feels like your  environment is what happens first and  then you move on after that i would say  this is another book that you can  audible johann has a lovely british  accent and there's some long stories  that are probably quite good to  visualize if you want something that you  need to listen to in the car or that you  can chill out to on an evening time this  is a good show next up we've got the war  of art by stephen pressfield this is  another short and brilliant read it  breaks down the challenges of motivation  and struggles as a creative it teaches  you how to overcome procrastination and  find your creativity and properly apply  yourself to your craft a lot of people  read this book because it helps them to  get past procrastination and overcome  creative hurdles but i read it and fell  in love with one chapter right in the  middle called turning pro it reminds us  that we need to treat the pursuit that  we are going after in our lives  seriously that we need to treat it like  a professional and not an amateur that  we need to lean in to the discomfort  that we have because that's what a  professional does it made me change the  entire process that i had in my approach  to the podcast and this youtube channel  because i thought well i want to be  really really good at this so what would  a professional do in fact i think that  so many other people enjoyed this  chapter that stephen went on to write an  entire second book called turning pro  about the exact same topic if you're in  a rut with life and you need a creative  kick up the ass  this is for you half of it feels like a  motivational david goggins monologue and  then the other half feels like a  spiritual book about the esoteric world  of higher meaning you have no reason not  to commit a couple of hours to reading  this next up we've got endurance by  alfred lansing this is the best  retelling of sir ernest shackleton's  voyage to the antarctic that i've ever  found it is a terrifying joy to read so  in 1914 shackleton and his team they  tried to cross the antarctic and pretty  much  everything that could go wrong does go  wrong they face insane odds in the  harshest conditions on the planet after  becoming stranded and having to survive  for more than two years the story tracks  the entire narrative in brutal detail  which makes for a powerful and inspiring  story for me i connected with it because  it reminded me just how far human  potential can go that when i'm facing  discomfort where i think my limit is is  probably not even halfway there is so  much headroom above that and hearing  this story from an analog age you know  1914 they left just after the first  world war was declared and when you find  out about stories like sir ernest  chuckleton and the entire team of guys  including a stowaway overcoming the odds  it really inspires you to go out there  and to take on the challenges of life  with a new set of eyes next up we've got  why we sleep by matthew walker as far as  i can see the world is split into two  camps the first one are people who do  understand the impact of sleep on your  health and your performance and the  second one are the people that don't i'm  a club promoter and for a long time i've  worked until three or four a.m multiple  nights per week so for me sleep was just  the annoying thing that i did in between  finishing work and then getting up to go  back to work again and this book is  single-handedly responsible for red  pilling me on how much my health and my  performance was suffering because i  wasn't prioritizing sleep i think this  is the only book that you need on sleep  in my opinion and if you don't  understand the effects on your health  and your longevity then dedicate some  time to it here you find out tips for  how to improve and optimize your sleep  insights into the function of why we do  it for the human body and a lot more  again this is probably one that you can  afford to audible you don't need to  necessarily take away any of the  statistics you just need to overall  adopt the mindset sleep is really really  important and the more that i do it the  better my performance will be finally  we've got models by mark manson this is  probably the only book that you need as  a man to become effective at dating so  manson went on to superstardom after  this but the book is still a classic it  breaks down the root behaviors that  cause female attraction and it gives  actionable and ethical advice that most  guys need to be successful at dating  like how can you overcome nervousness or  how do you keep conversations with women  engaging and interesting how do you find  a girl who you're genuinely attracted to  the reason that i really like this book  is because previously a lot of the  dating advice i'd got especially for men  was a bit icky it felt  quite  predatory in a way and it was all about  oh you've got to neg the beater and then  twist the lemon and tell her this and  whatever whatever i wasn't into that i  wanted to just understand the  fundamental principles and then allow my  personality to come through mark i spoke  to him on the podcast not long ago and  he said that this is exactly right the  purpose isn't to turn you into some  different sort of a person that can  sleep with a girl and then that doesn't  know what to say to her the next day  it's to become the sort of man that is  attractive to women again another book  that you can definitely afford to get on  audible you don't need to be writing  down too many notes just understand the  fundamentals of how attraction works and  then go out there and find your chick  let me know which of these books you  agree with and which ones i've missed  off in the comments below and don't  forget that if you want to get my full  reading list with 100 books that you  need to read before you die you can get  a copy for free by pressing right here  don't forget subscribe it makes me very  happy indeed  peace

